{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539631877,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE5MDUwMTcxMDg="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1539634063,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyOTk5NDYwOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-429994609"},"message":"\u003e anyhow exclude \"inputs\" from outputs?\n\nNo, there's currently no option to do that.\n\n\u003e May be it would somehow just work out because sourcedata/ is the subdataset, so it would not get unlocked recursively?\n\nYes, this setup should work because unlock isn't called recursively.  \n\n(The scenario you describe wasn't the reason I chose a non-recursive unlock.  I just went with it as the more conservative choice that could be revisited if someone argued for the recursive variant.)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539635502,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDAwMjI3Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430002276"},"message":"Cool, so ATM it would work because it is not recursive... but I can see that in some cases it would require recursive unlocking, so do you think that some additional option/way (exclusions for --input/output, e.g. `--output '^sourcedata` or even `--output '^{inputs}'`) could/should be implemented?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539635720,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDAwMzQ3OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430003479"},"message":"FWIW, initially I have used `!` instead of `^` as to exclusion, since I incorrectly remembered it being used by gitk as a way to exclude file paths, but it is `!`, quoting from IRC:\n```\n14:46  rafasc: yoh_: try gitk ... -- ':!path_to_exclude'\n14:47  rafasc: yoh_: or ':(exclude)path/to/exclude'\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1539639739,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDAyMTUyMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430021520"},"message":"\u003e I tried to recommend our YODA organization where we would do something like\n\nAnd YODA's separate \"outputs\" directory has to be dropped?\n\n\u003e but I can see that in some cases it would require recursive unlocking\n\nSure.  With a couple of *nested* input subdatasets, listing them out would be OK, but it could quickly become unmanageable.  I would like to see the case made with actual dataset examples, though.\n\n\u003e so do you think that some additional option/way (exclusions for --input/output, e.g. --output '^sourcedata or even --output '^{inputs}') could/should be implemented?\n\nCertainly could, and I'm not opposed to providing a way to do this.  I'm not sure what the best approach is.  I'm not thrilled about bloating the format string with more minilanguage inventions and wonder whether the less general approach of adding a flag to run which means \"filter inputs from the set of outputs\" would be preferable.\n\n\u003e FWIW, initially I have used ! instead of ^ as to exclusion, since I incorrectly remembered it being used by gitk as a way to exclude file paths, but it is !, quoting from IRC:\n\n`:^`, `:!`, and `:(exclude)` all mean the same thing.  See pathspec's entry of `man gitglossary`.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1539639739,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE0MDcwODQxMg=="},"target":"f139ef9381f8e978b7bd32a6c0d01f3fa8be368c736e86735a88de419a95f907","message":"\u003e I tried to recommend our YODA organization where we would do something like\n\nAnd YODA's separate \"outputs\" directory has to be dropped?\n\n\u003e but I can see that in some cases it would require recursive unlocking\n\nSure.  With a couple of *nested* input subdatasets, listing them out would be OK, but it could quickly become unmanageable.  I would like to see the case made with actual dataset examples, though.\n\n\u003e so do you think that some additional option/way (exclusions for --input/output, e.g. --output '^sourcedata or even --output '^{inputs}') could/should be implemented?\n\nCertainly could, and I'm not opposed to providing a way to do this.  I'm not sure what the best approach is.  I'm not thrilled about bloating the format string with more minilanguage inventions and wonder whether the less general approach of adding a flag to run which means \"filter inputs from the set of outputs\" would be preferable.\n\n\u003e FWIW, initially I have used ! instead of ^ as to exclusion, since I incorrectly remembered it being used by gitk as a way to exclude file paths, but it is !, quoting from IRC:\n\n`:^`, `:!`, and `:(exclude)` all mean the same thing.  See pathspec's entry in `man gitglossary`.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539696454,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDIzNjk0Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430236943"},"message":"I don't think this is a valid use case. There should always be multiple inputs and a single output dataset. Any deviation from that, that I can think of, can be organized in a way that is \"multiple inputs, single output\". If that is not the case, an output dataset cannot possibly have a clear link to all its inputs, hence any computation can not be reproducible (other than by chance, or some constraint that is outside the scope of any dataset). Both situation should be avoided.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539701023,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDI2NTMwNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430265307"},"message":"\u003e \u003e I tried to recommend our YODA organization where we would do something like\n\u003e \n\u003e And YODA's separate \"outputs\" directory has to be dropped?\n\nAFAIK YODA is a principle of keeping everything under git/git-annex and modular organization. Layouts we can suggest are not the only ones to follow.  Here it is a different layout - BIDS dataset, which defines 'sourcedata' as the input used to generate a given dataset.  I am just trying to address that use case.\n\n\u003e \u003e but I can see that in some cases it would require recursive unlocking\n\u003e \n\u003e Sure. With a couple of _nested_ input subdatasets, listing them out would be OK, but it could quickly become unmanageable. I would like to see the case made with actual dataset examples, though.\n\nI guess ATM I don't have it, but I can see where both `bids_raw` and `fmriprepped` datasets would be needed for an analysis, where `fmriprepped` wouldn't have all needed files, some of which would reside in the bids_raw, which could be under `fmriprepped/sourcedata` or at the same level, depending on organization\n\n\u003e \u003e so do you think that some additional option/way (exclusions for --input/output, e.g. --output '^sourcedata or even --output '^{inputs}') could/should be implemented?\n\u003e \n\u003e Certainly could, and I'm not opposed to providing a way to do this. I'm not sure what the best approach is. I'm not thrilled about bloating the format string with more minilanguage inventions and wonder whether the less general approach of adding a flag to run which means \"filter inputs from the set of outputs\" would be preferable.\n\nyes, I initially thought to suggest such a dedicated option as well, but I felt that more flexible and more generic way to exclude subpaths could be of greater benefit, e.g. where it is easier to say \"take all files as inputs but theses\" instead of demanding to list them all out explicitly while trying to avoid unnecessary ones (to avoid needless `get`) or simply \"incorrect\" (like here - input to be treated also as output).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539701390,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDI2Nzc5Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430267793"},"message":"\u003e I don't think this is a valid use case. There should always be multiple inputs and a single output dataset. Any deviation from that, that I can think of, can be organized in a way that is \"multiple inputs, single output\". If that is not the case, an output dataset cannot possibly have a clear link to all its inputs, hence any computation can not be reproducible (other than by chance, or some constraint that is outside the scope of any dataset). Both situation should be avoided.\n\nI really do not see why we should artificially restrict to have only a single output dataset - would only repel potential users and limit use cases. We already include in a subdataset's run record the original superdataset from which `run` was executed. Having outputs in multiple subdatasets nohow IMHO makes it less reproducible. May be I am missing the point?\nThe potential use case could be me having the \"study_analysis.sh\" script which produces output in both `preprocessed_data/` and `analysis_results/` or may be `analysis_results` and `analysis_results/preprocessed` sub-datasets. Another, more realistic usecase could be having too large of a \"results\" subdataset to hold all the subjects' results (HCP), so we might need to modularize it further (per subject).  Then results would fall into subdatasets per each subject.  Having \"only one output dataset\" would make it impossible.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539704430,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDI4NzUwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430287508"},"message":"None of these points contradict what I said, as far as I can see.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539706473,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDMwMDIxMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430300211"},"message":"Then I am not sure what your points are","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539707635,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDMwNjkxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430306917"},"message":"My single point is that any situation with more than one output ds is invalid, if you want to have reproducibility for every output dataset.\n\n\u003e We already include in a subdataset's run record the original superdataset from which run was executed.\n\nAnd the reason for that is that only the root dataset has functional linkage to any input dataset.\nIf you write outputs into another dataset, that dataset will not have all the information to reproduce its content.\n\nYou could somehow translate this, I am sure. But you could also simply have the output dataset be the root dataset -- **for a particular computation**.\n\nCompartmentalization is easily possible with a single output dataset -- just have multiple datasets with the same set of inputs, but each be the root dataset for the relevant operation. The datasets in the studyforrest project are highly compartmentalized, but never more than one output dataset at a time was needed.\n\nNothing prevents putting one or more of those \"output datasets\" into another dataset.\n\nThat being said, I do not oppose flexible description of input. I am just saying that the particular motivation you stated does not make this change a requirement.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539833238,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDg2MjkyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-430862925"},"message":"\u003e ... any situation with more than one output ds is invalid, if you want to have reproducibility for every output dataset.\n\nOk, I think I finally recovered from the short red-eye (+ medical emergency stop in Denver) flight... And let me with this even paraphrase your point:\n\n\u003e ... any situation with output ds not containing all the inputs is invalid.\n\nI.e., it is not about single-vs-plural output datasets, but that for guaranteed reproducibility of a dataset all necessary for that inputs (including environments) should be contained within it (could be within subdatasets).  Let's call it a \"YODA purity law\" and call such datasets \"Pure YODA\" datasets (studyforrest datasets could get the corresponding badge!)\n\nBut since we already support it (back reference to the super-dataset in the run record) there could be the \"purity of the 2nd kind\" (or \"impurity of the 1st kind\" ;)), where output dataset(s) (makes no difference if one or more) are produced as a result of an operation ran in their super-dataset.  It is then that super-dataset which would be the \"Pure YODA\" dataset (even though not containing outputs directly in it, but in subdatasets), since it would have access to all inputs/outputs.  It will be the one which could reproduce all known to it outputs.  Corresponding output datasets could then be called \"Impure YODA\" or \"Pure YODA of the 2nd kind\" or \"Pure YODA via Super\".\n\nI think it is somewhat crucial to allow for such cases. It could be needed because dataset boundaries might be dictated not by data hierarchy, but tech limitations (# of files in git, etc).  It might then be that pipeline produces 1000 files per each subject and there is 1000 subjects.  If there were no tech limitations, output dataset could just contain all the 1,000,000 output files and input dataset being its subdataset.  But currently we would need to split outputs to probably 1000 (one per each subject) or may be 10 (one per each modality if those divide more or less evenly?) subdatasets to make it possible to store outputs under datalad.  Moreover such subdivision of outputs (produced by a single pipeline) might be sensible in other cases just logically (e.g. as aforementioned \"modality\" case).  The \"overall\" output dataset would be the \"Pure YODA\" dataset, while each correponding sub-dataset \"Impure YODA\".  May be the \"Pure YODA\" dataset which actually produced some outputs in some sub-datasets could then be called \"Super-Pure YODA\" or \"Pure Super-YODA\"\n\nAll of that though is indeed largely orthogonal to the flexibility in desired input/output specification.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539833238,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE0MTc1NDA0OQ=="},"target":"b7e6868772de54df591091f6cdae43e1f223a870646b9c7f5ae6799d0d86f5e8","message":"\u003e ... any situation with more than one output ds is invalid, if you want to have reproducibility for every output dataset.\n\nOk, I think I finally recovered from the short red-eye (+ medical emergency stop in Denver) flight... And let me then re-phrase your point:\n\n\u003e ... any situation with output ds not containing all the inputs is invalid.\n\nI.e., it is not about single-vs-plural output datasets, but that for guaranteed reproducibility of a dataset all necessary for that inputs (including environments) should be contained within it (could be within subdatasets).  Let's call it a \"YODA purity law\" and call such datasets \"Pure YODA\" datasets (studyforrest datasets could get the corresponding badge!)\n\nBut since we already support it (back reference to the super-dataset in the run record) there could be the \"purity of the 2nd kind\" (or \"impurity of the 1st kind\" ;)), where output dataset(s) (makes no difference if one or more) are produced as a result of an operation ran in their super-dataset.  It is then that super-dataset which would be the \"Pure YODA\" dataset (even though not containing outputs directly in it, but in subdatasets), since it would have access to all inputs/outputs.  It will be the one which could reproduce all known to it outputs.  Corresponding output datasets could then be called \"Impure YODA\" or \"Pure YODA of the 2nd kind\" or \"Pure YODA via Super\".\n\nI think it is somewhat crucial to allow for such cases. It could be needed because dataset boundaries might be dictated not by data hierarchy, but tech limitations (# of files in git, etc).  It might then be that pipeline produces 1000 files per each subject and there is 1000 subjects.  If there were no tech limitations, output dataset could just contain all the 1,000,000 output files and input dataset being its subdataset.  But currently we would need to split outputs to probably 1000 (one per each subject) or may be 10 (one per each modality if those divide more or less evenly?) subdatasets to make it possible to store outputs under datalad.  Moreover such subdivision of outputs (produced by a single pipeline) might be sensible in other cases just logically (e.g. as aforementioned \"modality\" case).  The \"overall\" output dataset would be the \"Pure YODA\" dataset, while each correponding sub-dataset \"Impure YODA\".  May be the \"Pure YODA\" dataset which actually produced some outputs in some sub-datasets could then be called \"Super-Pure YODA\" or \"Pure Super-YODA\"\n\nAll of that though is indeed largely orthogonal to the flexibility in desired input/output specification.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1570266570,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzODYzMjMwMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2923#issuecomment-538632300"},"message":"Closing \"question\"-type issue with a response, and no further activity.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1570266570,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjY4OTE4NjUwMQ=="},"status":2}]}